An Operating System (OS) Simulator is a powerful educational tool that demonstrates various OS functions such as process management, memory management, scheduling, resource allocation, and synchronization in a visual and interactive way.​

Key OS Functions Demonstrated
Process Management: The simulator allows users to create, run, and monitor processes. It displays running, ready, and waiting processes, and each process is assigned a Process Control Block (PCB) to track its state and attributes.​

Memory Management: Users can experiment with memory allocation, deallocation, and observe how virtual memory and segmentation work. The simulator visually represents memory blocks and their usage.​

Scheduling Algorithms: The simulator supports various scheduling algorithms (e.g., FCFS, Round Robin, Priority Scheduling). Users can input burst times, arrival times, and see how processes are scheduled and executed.​​

Resource Allocation and Deadlock Handling: The simulator enables manual allocation and de-allocation of resources, allowing users to create and resolve deadlocks, and experiment with deadlock prevention and detection strategies.​

Synchronization and Critical Sections: Special constructs in the simulator allow users to explore synchronization, critical regions, and thread management, including the use of semaphores.​

File System and I/O Simulation: Some simulators include file system structure simulation, disk scheduling, and I/O operations, letting users see how files are managed and accessed.​

CPU and Memory Utilization Monitoring: The simulator provides real-time graphical views of CPU and memory utilization, helping users understand system performance and load balancing.​

How to Use an OS Simulator
Process Creation and Execution: Users can create processes, assign them priorities, and observe their lifecycle (creation, running, waiting, termination).​

Step-by-Step Simulation: Most simulators offer step, run, pause, and back buttons, allowing users to control the simulation and observe changes at each step.​

Visualization: The simulator displays process trees, PCBs, memory layouts, and scheduling queues, making it easy to understand the internal workings of the OS.​

Experimentation: Users can modify parameters (e.g., time slices, memory size, scheduling algorithms) and immediately see the effects on system behavior.​
